<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirambo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1840–1884</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirambo was a Nyamwezi ruler in present-day western Tanzania who built a powerful state, known as Urambo, in the 1870s. The son of a minor chief, he formed an army of ruga-ruga warriors armed with guns and conquered neighbouring chiefdoms west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>. From 1871 to 1875 he fought the Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders of Unyanyembe for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, and European visitors compared him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirambo.jpg" alt="Mirambo" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mnazini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Unyamwezi_c_1880s.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
